at times it can feel like everyone in  Britain is going on strike but how  significant is this industrial action in  a historical context and should we  really be bracing ourselves for another  winter of discontent let's take a look  at the number of working days lost to  strike action now this is the number of  workers on strike multiplied by the  number of days they've not worked and it  doesn't look that significant in recent  years not like the 2011 public sector  strikes or the 2014 public sector  strikes but it has been climbing during  the pandemic the line is flat because  statisticians stopped collecting the  data this chart it goes up to August  Borough expecting it to climb a lot  higher we know that up to 40 000 Railway  workers and up to 300 000 nurses are  braced to go on strike before Christmas  and these strikes they're going to be  pretty short-lived there's been a shift  towards shorter bursts of action between  one and three days nothing like the  1980s when you could see strike action  last well into a year and which sectors  are driving this increase well you may  have guessed it's the transport and  communication sectors that includes  Railway workers it includes postal  workers and the number of working days  lost to strike action in these sectors  it's at its highest since records began  in 2008. and why a workers going on  strike well quite simply because their  pay is not keeping up with inflation  here you can see the blue bars show real  wages rising and the red bars show real  wages falling that means wages are not  keeping up with inflation and since  November last year wages have been  falling so after a decade of wage  stagnation workers are not prepared to  see their living standards fall even  further but all of this strike action it  does come with a cost to the economy
 

